WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.500
MCP prompt templates are the third of the three MCP primitives, alongside tools and resources.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:13.165
They're reusable, parameterized prompt instructions stored on a server that users can select to

3
00:00:13.165 --> 00:00:15.129
pre-populate an interaction.

4
00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:21.635
In this lesson, we'll cover how templates are defined, how arguments work, and the scenarios where

5
00:00:21.635 --> 00:00:23.934
server-side prompts add real value.

6
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:31.000
The word prompt does a lot of work in this space, so it helps to pin down what it means inside MCP.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.215
An MCP prompt is one of the three MCP primitives, a reusable, parameterized template stored on a

8
00:00:38.215 --> 00:00:42.424
server that users select to pre-populate an interaction.

9
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:48.000
The critical word is user-controlled because a human explicitly chooses to apply it.

10
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
That is what separates it from resources, which the host injects, and tools, which the model invokes on its own.

11
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.500
Prompt arguments are the named, typed parameters the client fills in before the template is rendered.

12
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:09.274
And prompt rendering is the server-side step of substituting those argument values into the template

13
00:01:09.274 --> 00:01:12.932
body and returning the completed prompt to the client.

14
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Every MCP prompt template definition has three components.

15
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.500
The name is the stable identifier that clients use to request the template when they call prompts slash get.

16
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:29.303
The arguments list declares the named parameters the template expects, along with their types and

17
00:01:29.303 --> 00:01:30.440
any default values.

18
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.893
And the template body is the actual prompt text, with placeholders for each argument that gets

19
00:01:35.893 --> 00:01:37.442
substituted at render time.

20
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
When a client calls prompts slash get with the template name and a set of filled argument values,

21
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.500
the server substitutes those values into the body and returns the fully rendered prompt.

22
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:56.000
The client then injects that rendered prompt into the conversation as the opening instruction context.

23
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.500
Understanding how prompts differ from the other two MCP primitives matters for the exam.

24
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.500
Prompts are user-controlled.

25
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:09.100
The human explicitly selects a template from a list surfaced in the host UI, and the system

26
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.438
pre-populates the interaction with it.

27
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.000
Resources are application-controlled.

28
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
The host decides which data objects to retrieve and inject.

29
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
The user doesn't necessarily know it's happening.

30
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Tools are model-controlled.

31
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
The model decides when to invoke a tool based on what it needs to accomplish.

32
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Three primitives, three control points, user, application, and model.

33
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Keeping these distinctions clear is exactly what the Architect Foundation's exam tests in this area.

34
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.750
Server-side prompt templates are most valuable when task instructions are complex, long, or need to

35
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.931
be consistent across many users.

36
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.500
Multi-step analysis workflows benefit enormously from a structured template that enforces a repeatable methodology

37
00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.000
instead of relying on each user to retype a long instruction set.

38
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Compliance workflows are another strong use case.

39
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Approved instruction sets can be stored as templates, reviewed, and versioned,

40
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.000
which means users can't accidentally modify critical wording.

41
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.500
Onboarding flows for unfamiliar processes are a third category.

42
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.500
A well-designed template walks a user through a complex task

43
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:25.500
without requiring them to know the right prompting strategy ahead of time.

44
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.500
Versioning is one of the operational realities of prompt template management.

45
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.000
Three strategies cover most scenarios.

46
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The simplest is versioning in the name itself.

47
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.500
Append a version number like ReviewV2 so old and new templates coexist as separate entries in the server's prompt list.

48
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.839
The more flexible approach is passing version as a runtime argument and resolving the right template

49
00:03:50.839 --> 00:03:52.941
body server-side based on the value.

50
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.000
This keeps the registration count low while still supporting multiple versions simultaneously.

51
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:06.000
The third strategy is using a deprecation flag in the server response to mark old templates as retired,

52
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:13.000
which allows clients to warn users that the template will be removed without breaking existing workflows immediately.

53
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.500
Client-side access to prompt templates follows the same discovery pattern as resources.

54
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:26.500
A client starts by calling prompts slash list to retrieve all available templates along with their argument schemas.

55
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:31.500
The host UI surfaces these to the user as a selectable list.

56
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.500
When the user picks one, the host collects the required argument values,

57
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.500
validates them against the declared types if possible,

58
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:44.000
and calls prompts slash get with the template name and the filled argument values.

59
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.000
The server renders the final prompt by substituting the values and returns the complete text.

60
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.000
The client then injects that rendered prompt into the conversation.

61
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.500
The server handles all the rendering complexity.

62
00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:01.000
The client only needs to handle argument collection and injection.

63
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Argument design is where prompt template quality is won or lost.

64
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.500
Templates with too many required arguments create friction.

65
00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:15.500
Users have to fill in every field before they can get started, and the cognitive load reduces adoption.

66
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:22.500
The right approach is to keep required arguments minimal and provide sensible defaults for everything optional.

67
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.799
Typed arguments are worth the extra definition effort because they let clients validate input before

68
00:05:28.799 --> 00:05:30.437
calling prompts slash get,

69
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.500
which catches errors before they reach the server.

70
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Well-designed arguments make the same template applicable across many different contexts and user skill levels

71
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:47.000
without making the template body so generic that it loses its value as a standardized instruction.

72
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:54.000
One of the strongest arguments for server-side prompt templates is what they do for consistency and maintainability.

73
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
When every user who selects a template gets the same rendered instruction set,

74
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
the variance in model outputs drops significantly.

75
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.500
Compare that to a workflow where each user writes their own version of the same prompt.

76
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:12.500
You get different levels of detail, different structures, and different results.

77
00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.500
Maintenance is equally strong.

78
00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:21.500
Update the template body on the server once and every client picks up the change without any client-side code changes.

79
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:26.500
And for compliance-sensitive workflows, server-side templates can be formally reviewed,

80
00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:31.500
approved, and versioned before they're ever surfaced to users.

81
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.000
Prompt templates move hard-won instructions off individual users and onto a server,

82
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.000
so everybody runs the same version.

83
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.000
The reason that holds is that a person picks the template on purpose.

84
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.000
The model never reaches for one mid-task.

85
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:54.000
That deliberate selection is exactly why a template can afford to be long, detailed, and opinionated.

86
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.000
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

